I found 6 findings; the first two are the most serious. The nil converters in `toLSPDiagnostics` will crash the diagnostics request for any file that has a diagnostic. Declaration diagnostics are now computed in the LSP for the first time, and that exposes an emit-resolver checker lifetime problem that the comment in `internal/compiler/emitHost.go` already warns about.

I also looked at removing the explicit `Kind: lsproto.StringLiteralFull{}`. It changes nothing: the type is a zero-size struct and still sends `"full"`. Collecting declaration diagnostics even when semantic errors exist matches upstream TypeScript's language service, so I didn't flag either one. No CLAUDE.md rules apply to the changed Go file.
